# Customer Churn Prediction

Predicting which customers are likely to cancel their service so businesses can take proactive action to retain them.

## Dataset
- 5,000 customer records after cleaning
- Features include usage patterns, payment history, support tickets, and account details
- Target: Churn (left service or stayed)

## Problem Statement
Acquiring new customers costs more than retaining existing ones. This model helps identify at-risk customers early so retention teams can offer targeted incentives.

## Approach
1. Data cleaning and exploratory analysis
2. Feature engineering from behavioral and usage data
3. Handling class imbalance using SMOTE
4. Model training and evaluation
5. Interactive dashboard for business teams

## Models Used
- Logistic Regression
- Random Forest
- XGBoost
- Decision Tree

## Results

| Model | AUC-ROC | Recall | F1-Score |
|-------|---------|--------|----------|
| XGBoost | 0.84 | 0.78 | 0.76 |
| Random Forest | 0.82 | 0.75 | 0.74 |
| Logistic Regression | 0.76 | 0.68 | 0.69 |

## Key Improvements
- SMOTE improved recall from 0.62 to 0.78
- Feature engineering added 12 behavioral features
- Built Power BI dashboard for churn risk segmentation

## Key Findings
- Customers with frequent support tickets were 3x more likely to churn
- High usage in first month but drop-off in month 2 was a strong churn indicator
- Payment failures and price sensitivity were top predictors

## Technologies Used
- Python (Pandas, NumPy, Scikit-learn, XGBoost)
- Power BI for dashboard
- Matplotlib, Seaborn for visualizations
- SMOTE for handling imbalance

